𝟑𝟎 𝐃𝐚𝐲𝐬 𝐨𝐟 𝐀𝐧𝐬𝐢𝐛𝐥𝐞 – 𝐃𝐚𝐲 𝟏𝟒/𝟑𝟎

🏷️ 𝐀𝐧𝐬𝐢𝐛𝐥𝐞 𝐓𝐚𝐠𝐬 – 𝐑𝐮𝐧 𝐎𝐧𝐥𝐲 𝐭𝐡𝐞 𝐓𝐚𝐬𝐤𝐬 𝐘𝐨𝐮 𝐍𝐞𝐞𝐝

Imagine you have an Ansible playbook with 30 tasks, but you only want to update the Nginx configuration.

Would you run all 30 tasks? ❌

No. This is where 𝐀𝐧𝐬𝐢𝐛𝐥𝐞 𝐓𝐚𝐠𝐬 become useful. 🔥

Tags allow you to selectively execute specific tasks without running the entire playbook.

🔹 𝐁𝐚𝐬𝐢𝐜 𝐓𝐚𝐠 𝐄𝐱𝐚𝐦𝐩𝐥𝐞

- name: Install Nginx
  package:
    name: nginx
    state: present
  tags:
    - install

- name: Copy Nginx Configuration
  copy:
    src: nginx.conf
    dest: /etc/nginx/nginx.conf
  tags:
    - config

- name: Restart Nginx
  service:
    name: nginx
    state: restarted
  tags:
    - restart

Now you can run only the task you need.

🔹 𝐑𝐮𝐧 𝐚 𝐒𝐩𝐞𝐜𝐢𝐟𝐢𝐜 𝐓𝐚𝐠

Install:

ansible-playbook site.yml --tags install

Update configuration:

ansible-playbook site.yml --tags config

Restart service:

ansible-playbook site.yml --tags restart

🔹 𝐑𝐮𝐧 𝐌𝐮𝐥𝐭𝐢𝐩𝐥𝐞 𝐓𝐚𝐠𝐬

ansible-playbook site.yml --tags "install,config"

🔹 𝐒𝐤𝐢𝐩 𝐒𝐩𝐞𝐜𝐢𝐟𝐢𝐜 𝐓𝐚𝐬𝐤𝐬

ansible-playbook site.yml --skip-tags restart

This runs everything except tasks tagged restart.

🔍 𝐂𝐡𝐞𝐜𝐤 𝐀𝐯𝐚𝐢𝐥𝐚𝐛𝐥𝐞 𝐓𝐚𝐠𝐬

ansible-playbook site.yml --list-tags

You can also preview the tasks:

ansible-playbook site.yml --list-tasks

🌍 𝐑𝐞𝐚𝐥-𝐖𝐨𝐫𝐥𝐝 𝐔𝐬𝐞

In production, a playbook might contain:

✅ Package installation
✅ User creation
✅ Application deployment
✅ Configuration updates
✅ Service restarts
✅ Monitoring configuration

If you only need a configuration change, you can target it directly:

ansible-playbook production.yml --tags config

This makes changes faster, more controlled, and easier to troubleshoot.

⚠️ 𝐏𝐫𝐨𝐝𝐮𝐜𝐭𝐢𝐨𝐧 𝐓𝐢𝐩

Tags work best when combined with:

𝐑𝐨𝐥𝐞𝐬 + 𝐕𝐚𝐫𝐢𝐚𝐛𝐥𝐞𝐬 + 𝐓𝐚𝐠𝐬 + 𝐈𝐝𝐞𝐦𝐩𝐨𝐭𝐞𝐧𝐭 𝐓𝐚𝐬𝐤𝐬

🎯 𝐊𝐞𝐲 𝐓𝐚𝐤𝐞𝐚𝐰𝐚𝐲

𝐀𝐧𝐬𝐢𝐛𝐥𝐞 𝐓𝐚𝐠𝐬 𝐠𝐢𝐯𝐞 𝐲𝐨𝐮 𝐜𝐨𝐧𝐭𝐫𝐨𝐥 𝐨𝐯𝐞𝐫 𝐰𝐡𝐚𝐭 𝐫𝐮𝐧𝐬.

Instead of executing an entire playbook every time, you can target exactly the tasks you need.
